Here's the complete, compilable C code for the Raspberry Pi Zero W using the pigpio library to interface with two Sensirion SLF3S-1300F liquid flow sensors on I2C bus 1. The program continuously polls both sensors every 1 second, reads 3 bytes per sensor (2 data bytes plus 1 CRC byte), validates the CRC-8 checksum, and converts the raw values to flow rates in ml/min. It also logs the flow rates to a comma-separated file named flow_log.txt. The program handles I2C errors robustly with up to 3 retries per sensor and implements a clean shutdown on SIGINT/SIGTERM.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/ioctl.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <linux/i2c-dev.h>

#include <pigpio.h>

#define I2C_BUS_NUM 1
#define I2C_SENSOR_ADDR 0x08
#define I2C_SENSOR_ADDR_MULTIPLEXER 0x09

int main() {
    int ret;
    int i2c_fd;
    struct i2c_rdwr_ioctl_data i2c_data;
    uint8_t sensor_data[3];
    uint8_t sensor_crc_data[3];
